ing:\n    - Structural algorithms (connected-component labeling, flood-fill, boundary tracing, global operations)\n    - Avoid fragile pixelwise heuristics unless justified by all examples\n\n**5. Validation and Confidence**  \n- After generating your outputs for all test cases:\n    - **Visually and numerically compare** predicted outputs to the expected ones.\n    - **Explain why your transformation works** for *all* examples and why it does not overfit.\n    - State your **confidence (1\u201310)** in your solution and explain your reasoning.\n\n---\n\n**Guiding Principles:**\n- Always prioritize **explicit, structural pattern recognition** over guessing or local-only rules.\n- Ensure your explanation, plan, and code are **aligned and easily auditable**.\n- If the rule is simple (e.g., copying, filling), state so **succinctly and confidently**.\n\n---\n\n**Goal:**  \nProduce interpretable, correct solutions by carefully identifying the intended relationship between input and output grids, validating your rule, and demonstrating secure understanding of the transformation logic.\n\nFirst, return your reasoning about what the underlying rules and possible solution should be. Next, execute the tool calls to generate the output grid (do not output the grid directly). Once each tool call returns, the user will send the updated representation of the output grid. Continue with the tool calls until you are confident in your solution, then use the finish tool with a confidence score."
